        "The Things I Called Friendship was inspired by a true story from my own life. At the time, I did not fully understand my own feelings. Because homosexuality and queer identities were rarely discussed openly in the environment I grew up in, I tended to interpret my feelings as “just friendship.” It was only after experiencing an intimate relationship with another girl that I gradually realized that I could fall in love with girls, and that these feelings were real, not something I had simply imagined.",
        "At first, I was afraid to write this story. It was deeply personal, and I wondered whether sharing it might seem self-indulgent or whether an audience could connect with it. Before writing the script, I created two questionnaires, one for teenagers and another for parents and teachers. Their responses showed me that many young people were struggling with similar questions about identity, intimacy, and insecurity. I began to understand that a personal experience can become a theatrical story when it is shaped in a way that invites others to recognize their own feelings. I wanted queer teenagers to know that their emotions should not be dismissed as confusion or as “just a phase.” I also wanted to ask parents and teachers what young people lose when they are repeatedly encouraged to be “normal.”"
