        "The play follows Valerie, a sixteen- or seventeen-year-old who appears outgoing and funny, the kind of person who can bring a party to life. Beneath that confidence, however, she is sensitive and afraid of losing the people she loves. When she meets Lauren, the two grow close through everyday moments at school and eventually during a trip to Thailand. Valerie begins to sense that her feelings are more than friendship, but she does not know how to name them or whether she is ready to accept what they might mean. As rumors at school, family pressure, and Lauren’s reluctance to define their relationship create distance between them, Valerie becomes increasingly desperate for reassurance. When her mother suddenly decides to send her abroad, Valerie finally tells Lauren, “I love you.” The confession does not guarantee a happy ending. What matters is that Valerie stops using the word “friendship” to hide from a feeling she can no longer deny."
